p=A.v([1,A.v(["headerTitle","Day 1: What is an LED","question","Why does an LED only glow in one direction and need a resistor?","what","A light-emitting diode (LED) is a semiconductor that converts electricity into light in one direction (anode + to cathode \u2212).","like","Real-Life Analogy: Like a water pump pushing water through a narrow speed-valve to gently spin a one-way glowing waterwheel!","why","Cause & Effect: Without a 330 \u03a9 resistor, 85 mA rushes in and burns the LED; with 330 \u03a9, current stays at a safe 9.1 mA!","where","Real-World Places: Inside traffic signal lights, TV remote infrared transmitters, smartphone flashlights, and robot status lights!","when","When to Use It: Whenever an Arduino output pin goes HIGH (5V) to signal an alert, sensor detection, or power status!"],r,q),2,A.v(["headerTitle","Day 2: Resistors and Current Limiting","question","Why does an LED burn without a resistor?","what","A resistor limits the flow of electric current in a circuit, measured in Ohms (\u03a9), converting excess electrical energy safely.","like","Real-Life Analogy: Like a speed-bump on a road or a narrow pinch in a garden hose that keeps water pressure at a safe speed!","why","Cause & Effect: Ohm's Law (I = (5V \u2212 2V) \xf7 R) keeps current around 9.1 mA so delicate components never overheat!","where","Real-World Places: Inside phone chargers, traffic lights, smart TVs, and every Arduino LED & sensor circuit!","when","When to Use It: Always place a 220\u03a9\u2013330\u03a9 resistor in series whenever connecting a 2V LED to a 5V or 9V power source!"],r,q),3,A.v(["headerTitle","Day 3: Ground and Complete Circuits","question","Why won't a circuit work if the Ground (GND) wire is missing?","what","Ground (GND) is the 0V reference point that completes an electrical loop back to the battery or power source.","like","Real-Life Analogy: Like a circular water slide loop \u2014 water cannot keep flowing if the return pipe back to the pump is broken!","why","Cause & Effect: Open GND loop = 0.0 mA (dark LED); Closed GND loop = 9.1 mA continuous flow (glowing LED)!","where","Real-World Places: Car battery negative chassis ground, home appliance neutral/ground lines, and Arduino GND rails!","when","When to Use It: Always connect every component's negative (\u2212) return leg to a common GND rail before turning power ON!"],r,q),4,A.v(["headerTitle","Day 4: Digital Output Pins & Loops","question","How does Arduino control things on and off in a loop?","what","Digital pins can be switched HIGH (5V) or LOW (0V) using code. void loop() repeats these commands continuously.","like","Real-Life Analogy: Like a lighthouse light keeper flicking a switch ON and OFF to rhythmic count beats that repeat forever!","why","Cause & Effect: Without delay(), the loop toggles millions of times per second (a blur); delay(500) gives visible blinking!","where","Real-World Places: Turn signals on cars, pulsing warning beacons on radio towers, and robot indicator blinkers!","when","When to Use It: Whenever you want automated blinking, timed alerts, or periodic motor pulses without human intervention!"],r,q),5,A.v(["headerTitle","Day 5: Buttons and Digital Input","question","How does a microcontroller know when a button is pressed?","what","A push button connects or disconnects two points in a circuit. digitalRead() checks whether the pin sees HIGH or LOW.","like","Real-Life Analogy: Like a drawbridge: open = traffic stops (floating); closed = traffic crosses smoothly to GND!","why","Cause & Effect: Using INPUT_PULLUP prevents an erratic floating input by gently holding the pin at 5V until pressed to GND!","where","Real-World Places: Gamepad buttons, doorbells, keyboard keys, and emergency stop switches on factory machines!","when","When to Use It: Whenever a human needs to interact with your robot \u2014 starting a motor, firing a buzzer, or toggling lights!"],r,q),6,A.v(["headerTitle","Day 6: Buzzers + If-Else Decisions","question","How do buzzers make sound and how does code decide when to beep?","what","A piezo buzzer vibrates rapidly when fed an electrical pulse (PWM tone). If-else statements decide when to activate it.","like","Real-Life Analogy: Like a guard dog that only barks IF someone knocks on the front door, ELSE stays quietly asleep!","why","Cause & Effect: Tone frequency determines pitch (e.g. 880 Hz high chime vs 440 Hz low beep); if-condition governs triggering!","where","Real-World Places: Microwave oven beeps, car reverse parking sensors, digital alarm clocks, and doorbell chimes!","when","When to Use It: When you need audible feedback for danger, button clicks, sensor triggers, or user notifications!"],r,q),7,A.v(["headerTitle","Day 7: Variables & State Memory","question","How does a robot remember numbers, scores, and states?","what","Variables are named storage boxes in computer memory (e.g., int speed = 100; int distance = 15;) that hold values that change.","like","Real-Life Analogy: Like labelled lunchboxes or scoreboards on a soccer pitch that update every time a team scores a goal!","why","Cause & Effect: Without variables, code is hard-coded and static; with variables, robots adapt and remember previous measurements!","where","Real-World Places: Game high-scores, robot battery percentage counters, speedometer readings, and thermometer displays!","when","When to Use It: Whenever tracking sensor readings, counting button presses, or computing math before making decisions!"],r,q),8,A.v(["headerTitle","Day 8: Ultrasonic Distance Sensor (HC-SR04)","question",'How does a robot "see" walls and measure distance with sound?',"what","An HC-SR04 sensor transmits a 40 kHz ultrasonic burst from TRIG and measures the travel time until ECHO bounces back.","like","Real-Life Analogy: Like a bat flying in a dark cave or a submarine sonar pinging off undersea obstacles to navigate!","why","Cause & Effect: Speed of sound is 0.034 cm/\xb5s. Distance = (Echo Time in \xb5s \xd7 0.034) \xf7 2 (divided by 2 for round-trip)!","where","Real-World Places: Autonomous robot vacuum obstacle avoidance, car parking radar sensors, and smart automatic trash bins!","when","When to Use It: When your robot must avoid crashing into walls, detect approaching intruders, or measure liquid levels!"],r,q),9,A.v(["headerTitle","Day 9: What is Artificial Intelligence (AI)","question","How is AI different from regular computer programming?","what","Regular code follows rigid step-by-step rules; AI learns patterns from examples and data to make predictions.","like","Real-Life Analogy: Regular code is like following a baking recipe word-for-word; AI is like an experienced chef tasting and tuning!","why","Cause & Effect: With 10,000 photos of cats and dogs, an AI extracts visual features that no human could write in simple if-statements!","where","Real-World Places: Self-driving cars, voice assistants (Siri, Alexa), spam filters, and Learnix AI tutoring!","when","When to Use It: When rules are too complex, fuzzy, or variable for manual if-else statements (vision, speech, reasoning)!"],r,q),10,A.v(["headerTitle","Day 10: Image Recognition & Computer Vision","question","How do computers see and classify objects in camera images?","what","Images are grids of numeric pixels (RGB). Vision models detect edges, textures, and shapes to recognize objects.","like","Real-Life Analogy: Like building a LEGO picture: individual dots form edges, edges form shapes, and shapes become a recognizable face!","why","Cause & Effect: Neural networks pool pixel values into feature maps, letting a robot car tell pedestrians apart from stop signs!","where","Real-World Places: Face-unlock on smartphones, lane-keeping camera systems on cars, and sorting robots in recycling plants!","when","When to Use It: Whenever your robot needs a camera feed to recognize gestures, sort colored blocks, or track human motion!"],r,q)],t.S,t.P)
